"href":107},[19],"#midi-support","MIDI support",[33,110,111,112,43,116,120,121,47],{},"The MIDI part of the extension simulates a 128-key electronic\nkeyboard with ",[16,113,115],{"href":114},"#drum-names","47 drums",[16,117,119],{"href":118},"#instrument-names","128 melodic instruments",", as provided by\n",[16,122,125],{"href":123,"target":124},"https://www.midi.org/about-midi/gm/gm1_spec.shtml","_blank","General\nMIDI Level 1 specification",[33,127,128],{},"It supports 15 polyphonic instrument channels and a single percussion\nchannel. Using more than 15 different melodic instruments\nsimultaneously in a model will cause some sounds to be lost or cut\noff.",[33,130,131],{},"The pitch of a melodic instrument is specified by a key number. The\nkeys on the keyboard are numbered consecutively from 0 to 127, where\n0 is the left-most key. Middle C is key number 60.",[33,133,134],{},"The loudness of an instrument is specified by a velocity, which\nrepresents the force with which the keyboard key is depressed.\nVelocity ranges from 0 to 127, where 64 is the standard velocity. A\nhigher velocity results in a louder sound.",[23,136,139],{"id":137,"className":138},"primitives",[14],[16,140,143],{"className":141,"href":142},[19],"#primitives","Primitives",[33,145,146,147,47],{},"Looking for the primitive reference for the Sound extension? You can find ",[16,148,150],{"href":149},"/sound/dictionary","the full reference here",[152,153,157,158,165,166,171,172,165,179,171,182,165,189,171,192,165,199,171,202],"div",{"id":154,"className":155},"all",[156],"prose","\n  ",[16,159,162],{"className":160,"href":161},[40],"#drums",[40,163,164],{},"sound:drums","\n   ",[67,167,170],{"className":168},[169],"font-bold"," · "," \n  ",[16,173,176],{"className":174,"href":175},[40],"#instruments",[40,177,178],{},"sound:instruments",[67,180,170],{"className":181},[169],[16,183,186],{"className":184,"href":185},[40],"#play-drum",[40,187,188],{},"sound:play-drum",[67,190,170],{"className":191},[